{
  "coreName": "Dolphin",
  "author": "the Dolphin Team",
  "url": "https://github.com/dolphin-emu/dolphin",
  "machineSetting": "machine",
  "machines": [
    {
      "id": "GC",
      "label": "GameCube",
      "when": [
        "gamecube"
      ],
      "extensions": {
        ".iso": "GC",
        ".gcm": "GC",
        ".rvz": "GC",
        ".dol": "GC",
        ".elf": "GC"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "Wii",
      "label": "Wii",
      "when": [
        "wii"
      ],
      "extensions": {
        ".iso": "Wii",
        ".rvz": "Wii"
      }
    }
  ],
  "romFile": "game",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    256,
    16,
    64,
    256,
    2560
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. The GameCube itself is small (24MB main RAM + 16MB ARAM + caches, all anonymous mmaps since the sandbox has no shared memory), but dolphin's own working set - the software rasterizer's EFB, texture and vertex machinery, DiscIO buffers - lives in the mmap arena too, which musl routes every large allocation into.",
  "video": {
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame, which follow the VI field being scanned out (640x480 NTSC progressive, 640x287-288 as one PAL/NTSC interlaced field). The static vsync fraction is the NTSC default; the runtime GetVsyncNumerator/Denominator exports follow the machine's actual video mode, PAL included.",
    "width": 720,
    "height": 574,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 480,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "the mixer's 48kHz stereo output, drained by machine time: rate * den / num samples per field with the remainder accumulated, so a PAL field is exactly 960 pairs and nothing is ever lost or invented.",
    "samplesPerFrame": 16384,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "input": {
    "name": "GameCube Controller",
    "_comment": "Four controller ports, each the pad a GameCube ships with. Wire order per pad: A B X Y Z Start Up Down Left Right L R, then Main Stick X/Y, C-Stick X/Y and the two analog trigger travels. The digital L/R bits are the full-press clicks at the bottom of the trigger travel; pressing them reports full analog travel too, the way the real trigger cannot click without being down.",
